 ],
 "literal": "Disgrace if comes, who do not live, honour those who possess, [their] glory, having worshipped, will praise, the world.",
 "translation": "The world will worship and praise the bright glory of those who possess such honour that they would not choose to live if disgrace befell them.",
 "modern": "அவமானம் வந்தால் உயிர் வாழாத மானம் உடையவர்களுடைய புகழை, இவ்வுலகம் தொழுது போற்றும்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet highlights the supreme value of honour and self-respect. It describes people who possess such high self-esteem that they prefer death over a life of disgrace (iḷi). The poet states that the world (ulaku) does not just remember these individuals, but actively bows down to (toḻutu) and extols (ēttum) their lasting glory (oḷi). The word 'oḷi' here represents the radiant, luminous fame that outlives their physical existence.",
